      "theory_id": "secondary_sc_ptsd_nexus",
      "theory_name": "Secondary Service Connection via PTSD Aggravation",
      "legal_framework": {
        "primary_statute": "38 CFR § 3.310",
        "standard": "Any increase in severity of a nonservice-connected disease or injury that is proximately due to or the result of a service-connected disease or injury will be service connected for the degree of aggravation.",
        "requirements": [
          "Service-connected PTSD (assumed established)",
          "Sleep apnea existed but was nonservice-connected",
          "Proximate causal link: PTSD aggravated/caused the sleep apnea"
        ]
      },
      "supporting_evidence_needed": [
        "Competent medical nexus opinion linking PTSD symptoms (hyperarousal, sleep disruption, nightmares) to sleep apnea onset or worsening",
        "Temporal relationship: sleep apnea diagnosis or worsening after PTSD service connection",
        "Evidence that PTSD-related hypervigilance or behavioral changes increased apnea severity",
        "If sleep apnea pre-existed: medical evidence showing aggravation due to PTSD",
        "VA examination findings on causation (was exam adequate?)"
      ],
      "current_evidence_gaps": [
        "**Medical nexus gap**: denial likely indicates absence of competent medical opinion establishing proximate causation from PTSD",
        "**Exam adequacy**: unclear whether VA examiner specifically addressed the PTSD-to-apnea causal chain or only rated apnea in isolation",
        "**Mechanism evidence**: missing evidence of *how* PTSD symptoms (specific manifestations) caused/aggravated apnea (e.g., arousal-related, behavioral)"
      ],
      "calibration": "This theory would require development of a competent medical nexus opinion establishing proximate causation. The burden is on the veteran to prove the link—the corpus alone does not establish it. Evidence development could include VA examination request specifically targeting the PTSD-apnea nexus."
